<section xml:id="sec-mod9-practice-set">
  <title>Module 9: Practice Problem Set</title>
  <p>Estimated time: 100 minutes</p>

    <p>
      Problems are labeled <m>[\mathbf{F}]</m> (Foundational),
      <m>[\mathbf{S}]</m> (Standard), and <m>[\mathbf{C}]</m>
      (Challenge). Work in order within each level; later problems
      often build on earlier ones.
    </p>
    <p>
      These problems are not submitted, but write out complete solutions.
      For any problem asking you to <em>prove</em> something, apply the
      standards from the Worked Examples: strategy stated up front,
      complete sentences, every step justified, and each theorem cited
      by name.
    </p>

  <!-- ============================================================
       FOUNDATIONAL
  ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod9-ps-f">
    <title>Foundational Problems</title>
    <p>
      These problems practice the basic moves of the module: finding
      pointwise limits of sequences of functions, computing sup-norm
      distances to the pointwise limit, deciding whether convergence is
      uniform on a given set, and applying the Weierstrass <m>M</m>-Test to
      series of functions.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod9-ps-f1">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{F}]</m> Pointwise Limits</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the pointwise limit of each sequence of functions on the
        given set. No proof of convergence is required; a sentence of
        justification is enough.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>f_n(x) = \dfrac{1}{1 + nx}</m> on <m>(0, \infty)</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>f_n(x) = \dfrac{nx}{1 + nx}</m> on <m>[0, \infty)</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>f_n(x) = \dfrac{\sin(nx)}{n}</m> on <m>\R</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>f_n(x) = \left(1 + \dfrac{x}{n}\right)^n</m> on
              <m>\R</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) For <m>x \gt 0</m>: <m>nx \to \infty</m>, so
        <m>1/(1+nx) \to 0</m>. Pointwise limit: <m>f \equiv 0</m> on
        <m>(0,\infty)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (b) At <m>x = 0</m>: <m>f_n(0) = 0</m> for all <m>n</m>, so
        <m>f(0) = 0</m>. For <m>x \gt 0</m>: <m>nx/(1+nx) = 1/(1/nx + 1)
        \to 1</m>. Pointwise limit: <m>f(0)=0</m>, <m>f(x)=1</m> for
        <m>x \gt 0</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (c) <m>|\sin(nx)/n| \leq 1/n \to 0</m>. Pointwise limit:
        <m>f \equiv 0</m> on <m>\R</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (d) Standard limit: <m>(1+x/n)^n \to e^x</m>. Pointwise limit:
        <m>f(x) = e^x</m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod9-ps-f2">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{F}]</m> Sup-Norm Computations</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For each of the first three sequences in F1, compute
        <m>S_n = \sup_{x \in D} |f_n(x) - f(x)|</m>
        (where <m>f</m> is the pointwise limit from F1) and determine
        whether <m>S_n \to 0</m>.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            <p>
              Part (a) of F1, on <m>(0, \infty)</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Part (b) of F1, on <m>[0, \infty)</m> and on <m>[1, \infty)</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Part (c) of F1, on <m>\R</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) <m>|f_n(x) - 0| = 1/(1+nx)</m>; <m>\sup_{x \gt 0} 1/(1+nx)
        = 1</m> (approaching as <m>x \to 0^+</m>). <m>S_n = 1</m> for
        all <m>n</m>. <em>Not</em> uniform on <m>(0,\infty)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (b) On <m>[0,\infty)</m>: at <m>x=0</m>, <m>|f_n(0)-0|=0</m>;
        for <m>x \gt 0</m>, <m>|f_n(x)-1| = 1/(1+nx) \leq 1</m>, approaching
        <m>1</m> as <m>x \to 0^+</m>. So <m>S_n = 1</m>. Not uniform.
        On <m>[1,\infty)</m>: <m>\sup_{x \geq 1} 1/(1+nx) = 1/(1+n)
        \to 0</m>. Uniform on <m>[1,\infty)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (c) <m>S_n = \sup_\R |\sin(nx)/n| = 1/n \to 0</m>. Uniform on
        <m>\R</m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod9-ps-f3">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{F}]</m> Uniform Convergence: Quick Checks</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For each, state whether the convergence is uniform on the given
        set, and give a one-sentence justification.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>f_n(x) = x/n</m> on <m>[0,1]</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>f_n(x) = x/n</m> on <m>[0, \infty)</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>f_n(x) = x^2/(x^2 + n)</m> on <m>\R</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>f_n(x) = e^{-nx}</m> on <m>(0, \infty)</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) <m>\sup_{[0,1]} |x/n| = 1/n \to 0</m>. Uniform.
      </p>
      <p>
        (b) <m>\sup_{[0,\infty)} |x/n| = \infty</m> for every <m>n</m>
        (unbounded domain). Not uniform.
      </p>
      <p>
        (c) <m>|x^2/(x^2+n)| \leq x^2/n</m>; but <m>x^2/n</m> is
        unbounded on <m>\R</m>. More directly: <m>\sup_{x \in \R}
        x^2/(x^2+n) = 1</m> for all <m>n</m> (limit as <m>x \to \infty</m>).
        Not uniform on <m>\R</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (d) Pointwise limit is <m>0</m>. <m>\sup_{x \gt 0} e^{-nx} = 1</m>
        (as <m>x \to 0^+</m>). Not uniform on <m>(0,\infty)</m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod9-ps-f4">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{F}]</m> <m>M</m>-Test Warm-Up</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Apply the Weierstrass <m>M</m>-Test to each series. State the
        bound <m>M_n</m> and verify <m>\sum M_n \lt \infty</m>.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{x^n}{n!}</m>
              on <m>[-5,5]</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{n^2 + x^2}</m>
              on <m>\R</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \left(\frac{x}{2}\right)^n</m>
              on <m>[-1,1]</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) <m>M_n = 5^n/n!</m>; <m>\sum M_n</m> converges by the Ratio
        Test (the ratio <m>M_{n+1}/M_n = 5/(n+1) \to 0</m>), and in fact
        <m>\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} 5^n/n! = e^5 - 1 \lt \infty</m> (the series
        for <m>e^5</m> without its <m>n = 0</m> term).
        Uniform on <m>[-5,5]</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (b) <m>1/(n^2+x^2) \leq 1/n^2 =: M_n</m> for all <m>x \in \R</m>;
        <m>\sum 1/n^2 \lt \infty</m>. Uniform on <m>\R</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (c) <m>|(x/2)^n| \leq (1/2)^n =: M_n</m> on <m>[-1,1]</m>;
        <m>\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} (1/2)^n = 2 \lt \infty</m>. Uniform on <m>[-1,1]</m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================
       STANDARD
  ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod9-ps-s">
    <title>Standard Problems</title>
    <p>
      These problems apply the same moves to longer arguments: a
      sup-norm proof of uniform convergence, a sequence-of-points proof
      that convergence is not uniform, uniform convergence and term-by-term
      integration of a geometric series, radii of convergence from the
      Ratio Test followed by the <m>M</m>-Test, and an example where
      uniform convergence does not carry over to derivatives.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod9-ps-s1">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{S}]</m> Proving Uniform Convergence</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove that <m>f_n(x) = x^n/n</m> converges uniformly to
        <m>0</m> on <m>[0,1]</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Your proof must: (i) identify the pointwise limit; (ii) compute
        <m>\sup_{x \in [0,1]} |f_n(x) - 0|</m> exactly; (iii) show this
        sup tends to <m>0</m> and invoke the sup-norm criterion.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        On <m>[0,1]</m>, <m>x^n \leq 1</m>, so
        <m>\sup_{[0,1]} x^n/n \leq 1/n</m>.
      </p>
    </hint>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod9-ps-s2">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{S}]</m> Non-Uniform: Using a Sequence</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove that <m>f_n(x) = nx\,e^{-nx}</m> does <em>not</em> converge
        uniformly to <m>0</m> on <m>(0, \infty)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Your proof must exhibit a sequence of points <m>x_n \in (0,\infty)</m>
        such that <m>|f_n(x_n) - 0| \not\to 0</m>, thereby showing
        <m>\sup_{(0,\infty)} |f_n(x)| \not\to 0</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Find the maximizer of <m>nx\,e^{-nx}</m> by taking the derivative
        in <m>x</m> and setting it to zero. The max is at <m>x = 1/n</m>
        with value <m>e^{-1}</m>.
      </p>
    </hint>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod9-ps-s3">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{S}]</m> Term-by-Term Integration</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>f_n(x) = \sum_{k=0}^{n} (-1)^k x^{2k}</m> be the <m>n</m>th
        partial sum of the geometric series for <m>1/(1+x^2)</m>.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            <p>
              Show that <m>f_n \to 1/(1+x^2)</m> uniformly on <m>[-r,r]</m>
              for any <m>0 \lt r \lt 1</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Use term-by-term integration to compute
              <m>\int_0^x f_n(t)\,dt</m> and take the limit as
              <m>n \to \infty</m> to recover the formula
              <m>\arctan(x) = \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} \frac{(-1)^k x^{2k+1}}{2k+1}</m>
              for <m>|x| \lt 1</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod9-ps-s4">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{S}]</m> Radius of Convergence and Uniform Convergence</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the radius of convergence <m>R</m> of each power series
        <m>\sum a_n x^n</m> using the Ratio Test. Then, for each, determine
        the values of <m>r \gt 0</m> for which the <m>M</m>-Test, applied
        with <m>M_n = |a_n| r^n</m> (the largest value of
        <m>|a_n x^n|</m> on <m>[-r,r]</m>), guarantees uniform convergence
        on <m>[-r,r]</m>. In particular, decide whether <m>r = R</m> is
        allowed.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{x^n}{n \cdot 3^n}</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} n!\,x^n</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{x^n}{n^2 + 1}</m>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) Ratio <m>\to |x|/3</m>. <m>R = 3</m>. Uniform on
        <m>[-r,r]</m> for any <m>0 \lt r \lt 3</m> (by <m>M</m>-Test with
        <m>M_n = r^n/(n \cdot 3^n) \leq (r/3)^n</m>, a convergent geometric
        bound). The value <m>r = 3</m> is not allowed: there
        <m>M_n = 1/n</m>, and the harmonic series diverges. (In fact the
        series itself diverges at <m>x = 3</m>.) No <m>r \gt 3</m> works
        either: for <m>|x| \gt 3</m> the ratio tends to
        <m>|x|/3 \gt 1</m>, so the series diverges at <m>x = r</m> by the
        Ratio Test. In particular <m>\sum M_n</m>, which is the series at
        <m>x = r</m>, diverges, and the series cannot converge uniformly on
        <m>[-r,r]</m>, since it does not even converge at <m>x = r</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (b) Ratio <m>= (n+1)|x| \to \infty</m> for any <m>x \neq 0</m>.
        <m>R = 0</m>: the series converges only at <m>x = 0</m>. No
        <m>r \gt 0</m> works, since the series diverges at every point of
        <m>[-r,r]</m> other than <m>0</m>; the only set on which the series
        converges (trivially uniformly) is <m>\{0\}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (c) Ratio <m>\to |x|</m>. <m>R = 1</m>. Here <m>r = R</m> is
        allowed: for <m>0 \lt r \leq 1</m>,
        <m>M_n = r^n/(n^2 + 1) \leq 1/(n^2 + 1)</m>, and
        <m>\sum 1/(n^2+1)</m> converges by comparison with
        <m>\sum 1/n^2</m>. So the series converges uniformly on
        <m>[-1,1]</m>, and hence on every <m>[-r,r]</m> with
        <m>r \leq 1</m>. No <m>r \gt 1</m> works, since the series
        diverges at <m>x = r</m> (its terms do not tend to <m>0</m>).
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod9-ps-s5">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{S}]</m> Uniform Convergence and Differentiation</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>f_n(x) = \sin(nx)/\sqrt{n}</m> on <m>\R</m>.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            <p>
              Show that <m>f_n \to 0</m> uniformly on <m>\R</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Compute <m>f_n'(x)</m>. Does <m>f_n'(x)</m> converge
              pointwise to <m>0</m>?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              What does this example show about whether uniform convergence
              of <m>(f_n)</m> implies convergence of <m>(f_n')</m>?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) <m>\sup_\R |\sin(nx)/\sqrt{n}| \leq 1/\sqrt{n} \to 0</m>.
        Uniform.
      </p>
      <p>
        (b) <m>f_n'(x) = \sqrt{n}\cos(nx)</m>. At <m>x = 0</m>:
        <m>f_n'(0) = \sqrt{n} \to \infty</m>. So <m>f_n'</m> does not
        converge to <m>0</m> pointwise at <m>x = 0</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (c) Uniform convergence of <m>(f_n)</m> does NOT imply convergence
        of the derivatives <m>(f_n')</m>. Differentiating term by term
        requires an additional condition (e.g., uniform convergence of the
        derivatives; this is a theorem for MAT 5610).
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================
       CHALLENGE
  ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod9-ps-c">
    <title>Challenge Problems</title>
    <p>
      These problems extend the module's ideas: a proof that a uniform
      limit of Riemann integrable functions on <m>[a,b]</m> is integrable,
      with integral equal to the limit of the integrals, and a proof using
      the Weierstrass <m>M</m>-Test that a power series with positive
      radius of convergence <m>R</m> converges uniformly on each interval
      <m>[-r,r]</m> with <m>0 \lt r \lt R</m>.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod9-ps-c1">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{C}]</m> Proof of the Term-by-Term Integration Theorem</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove: if <m>f_n \to f</m> uniformly on <m>[a,b]</m> and each
        <m>f_n</m> is Riemann integrable on <m>[a,b]</m>, then <m>f</m>
        is Riemann integrable and
        <m>\int_a^b f = \lim_{n \to \infty} \int_a^b f_n</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Your proof must use the following steps: (i) show <m>f</m> is
        bounded (since each <m>f_n</m> is bounded and convergence is
        uniform); (ii) show <m>f</m> is Riemann integrable by the
        integrability criterion: prove that for every partition <m>P</m>
        and every <m>N</m>,
        <md>U(f,P) - L(f,P) \leq U(f_N,P) - L(f_N,P) + 2(b-a)\sup_x |f_N(x) - f(x)|,</md>
        and use this to produce, for each <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>, a
        partition <m>P</m> with <m>U(f,P) - L(f,P) \lt \varepsilon</m>;
        (iii) estimate
        <m>\left|\int_a^b f_n - \int_a^b f\right| \leq
        (b-a) \cdot \sup_x |f_n(x) - f(x)|</m>;
        (iv) conclude by the definition of limit.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        For step (ii), compare the supremum and infimum of <m>f</m> and of
        <m>f_N</m> on each subinterval: if <m>|f_N - f| \leq s</m> on
        <m>[a,b]</m>, the supremum of <m>f</m> on a subinterval is at most
        the supremum of <m>f_N</m> plus <m>s</m>, and the infimum is at
        least the infimum of <m>f_N</m> minus <m>s</m>. Given
        <m>\varepsilon</m>, choose <m>N</m> first (so that
        <m>2(b-a)\sup_x|f_N(x) - f(x)| \lt 2\varepsilon/3</m>), and then
        choose <m>P</m> using the integrability of <m>f_N</m>.
        For step (iii), use
        <m>\left|\int_a^b (f_n - f)\right| \leq \int_a^b |f_n - f|
        \leq (b-a) \cdot \sup_x |f_n(x) - f(x)|</m>.
        For step (i): there exists <m>N</m> such that
        <m>|f_n(x) - f(x)| \lt 1</m> for all <m>n \geq N</m> and
        <m>x \in [a,b]</m>; so <m>|f(x)| \leq |f_N(x)| + 1 \leq M + 1</m>
        where <m>M</m> bounds <m>f_N</m>.
      </p>
    </hint>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod9-ps-c2">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{C}]</m> Uniform Convergence of a Power Series</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} a_n x^n</m> be a power series with
        radius of convergence <m>R \gt 0</m>. Prove that for any
        <m>0 \lt r \lt R</m>, the series converges uniformly on
        <m>[-r, r]</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Your proof must: (i) observe that <m>|a_n x^n| \leq |a_n| r^n</m>
        for <m>x \in [-r,r]</m>; (ii) show <m>\sum |a_n| r^n \lt \infty</m>
        (use the definition of radius of convergence: because
        <m>r \lt R</m>, there is a point <m>x_1</m> with
        <m>|x_1| \gt r</m> at which <m>\sum a_n x_1^n</m> converges;
        compare <m>|a_n| r^n</m> with a geometric series);
        (iii) apply the Weierstrass <m>M</m>-Test with <m>M_n = |a_n| r^n</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        The key fact: since <m>\sum a_n x_1^n</m> converges, its terms
        tend to <m>0</m>, and a convergent sequence is bounded, so there is
        <m>C</m> with <m>|a_n x_1^n| \leq C</m> for all <m>n</m>. Put
        <m>r' = |x_1|</m>. Then
        <m>|a_n| r^n = |a_n| (r')^n \cdot (r/r')^n \leq C (r/r')^n</m>,
        and <m>\sum (r/r')^n \lt \infty</m> because
        <m>0 \lt r/r' \lt 1</m>. The Comparison Test finishes step (ii).
      </p>
    </hint>
  </exercise>

</section>
